      "value": "@{62770b2ab9aa4552a0459dc1|full_name}\n\nAh yes, that’s completely understandable.\n\nIf you’re okay with using them, you could use an LLM to have it whip up an emulator in WebGL for you. You can then export the design mockup as an SVG, and have it be displayed at a variety of distances. You can then add things like the infamous screen door effect (for certain headsets), different resolutions, adding a slight blur, etc. You’re also able to add a variety of backgrounds to test readability against them to see how your design performs\n\nIf you really want to go all out, you could have it where it supports WebXR, but this is additional work and not really necessary for the design process, a simulated environment is enough to work from and attain valuable feedback. \n\nThat being said, keep in mind of FOV of your emulated camera, it’s hard to quantifiably come up with a solution for what your emulated camera FOV should be, but in general, objects will appear larger than they actually are.\n\nIt would not be 1:1 to Unity, but it would provide a valuable simulator of what it may look like in a VR environment.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
